---
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { SITE, INSTALL } from '../../data/site';

const toc = [
  { id: 'requirements', text: 'Requirements' },
  { id: 'load', text: 'Load the extension' },
  { id: 'key', text: 'Add a model key' },
  { id: 'verify', text: 'Verify it works' },
  { id: 'agent', text: 'Connect an agent (optional)' },
  { id: 'update', text: 'Update and uninstall' },
  { id: 'trouble', text: 'Troubleshooting' },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'HowTo',
    name: 'Install HailBrowser in Chrome',
    description: 'Load the HailBrowser extension unpacked, add a model key, and verify the side panel agent runs.',
    totalTime: 'PT2M',
    tool: [{ '@type': 'HowToTool', name: 'Google Chrome, Edge or Brave (Chromium 116+)' }],
    step: [
      { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Clone the repository', text: 'git clone https://github.com/Shyamnath-Sankar/project-hailbrowser.git, then npm install inside the checkout.' },
      { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Load the extension folder', text: 'Open chrome://extensions, enable Developer mode, click Load unpacked and choose the extension/ folder.' },
      { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Open the side panel', text: 'Click the toolbar icon or press Alt+H, then open Settings.' },
      { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Add a model key', text: 'Pick a provider in Settings and paste its API key. Keys stay in chrome.storage.local.' },
      { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Run a first goal', text: 'Ask the side panel to read the current tab: it should report the page title and its interactive elements.' },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${SITE.url}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Docs', item: `${SITE.url}/docs` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Install', item: `${SITE.url}/docs/install` },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Doc
  title="Install HailBrowser in Chrome"
  description="Load the HailBrowser extension unpacked, add your own model key, and verify the side panel agent runs. Node 18+, Chromium 116+, no build step."
  label="Docs · Start"
  lead="There is no store listing and no account. You clone the repository, point Chrome at the extension folder, and give the side panel a model key."
  toc={toc}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A desktop Chromium browser, version {SITE.minChrome} or newer: Chrome, Edge, Brave, Arc, Vivaldi.</li>
    <li>Node 18 or newer, for the clone-and-install step and for the MCP server if you connect an agent.</li>
    <li>An API key for one model provider, for the side panel. Coding agents can skip this if you configure a decision model instead.</li>
    <li>git, or a zip of the repository. Nothing else: the extension is plain ES modules with no build step.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <p class="label">What you are installing</p>
    <p>
      One unpacked extension, plus a Node process that only runs while your MCP client is open. There is
      no background service on your machine beyond Chrome's own extension host.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="load">1 · Load the extension</h2>
  <p>Clone the repository and install the workspace. The install is only needed for the MCP server package; the extension folder itself has no dependencies.</p>

  <CodeBlock code={INSTALL.clone} file="terminal" lang="bash" label="Clone and install" />

  <p>Then load the folder Chrome-style:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <code>chrome://extensions</code>.</li>
    <li>Turn on <strong>Developer mode</strong>, top right.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Load unpacked</strong> and pick the <code>extension/</code> folder inside your checkout. Pick the folder itself, not a file inside it.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    The toolbar icon opens the side panel. <code>Alt+H</code> does the same from the keyboard. Pin the
    icon if you plan to use it daily; Chrome hides new extensions behind the puzzle menu otherwise.
  </p>

  <h2 id="key">2 · Add a model key</h2>
  <p>
    Open the side panel, then <strong>Settings</strong>. HailBrowser talks to any provider you already
    pay for, and to local runtimes that need no key at all.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Key needed</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Anthropic</td><td>Yes</td><td>Claude models, tool calling.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>OpenAI, OpenRouter, Together, Groq, DeepSeek</td><td>Yes</td><td>Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint works.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Google Gemini</td><td>Yes</td><td>Fast models suit long task loops.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ollama, LM Studio</td><td>No</td><td>Local. For Ollama start it with <code>OLLAMA_ORIGINS=chrome-extension://*</code>, otherwise the extension gets a 403.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Keys are stored in <code>chrome.storage.local</code> and sent only to the provider you selected.
    There is no HailBrowser server in the middle, and no telemetry.
  </p>

  <h2 id="verify">3 · Verify it works</h2>
  <p>Open any normal page, open the panel, and ask something that needs the page:</p>
  <blockquote>What does this page say it costs, and is there a cheaper plan below?</blockquote>
  <p>
    A working install answers from the page and shows the tool calls it made. If the panel answers
    without touching the page, the model is running but the extension is not attached; check that the
    tab is a normal <code>https://</code> page and not a <code>chrome://</code> one.
  </p>
  <p>Two more checks worth doing once:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>Alt+H</code> opens the panel from the keyboard.</li>
    <li>Settings → Safety has <strong>Allow external agents</strong> on if you plan to connect a coding agent.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="agent">4 · Connect an agent (optional)</h2>
  <p>
    If you want Claude Code, OpenCode, Cursor, Codex, Windsurf, Gemini CLI or Claude Desktop to drive the
    browser, print the config with your real checkout path:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={INSTALL.setup} file="terminal" lang="bash" label="Print every client config" />
  <p>
    Paste the block for your client, restart it, and look for a green <strong>MCP connected</strong> label
    in the side panel header. The details per client are on
    <a href="/docs/connect">connect an agent</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="update">5 · Update and uninstall</h2>
  <p>
    Pull the repository, then press the reload arrow on the HailBrowser card in
    <code>chrome://extensions</code>. Reinstalling from a different folder changes the extension ID, which
    the MCP server treats as a new extension: run <code>npx hailbrowser-mcp trust &lt;new id&gt;</code>, or
    <code>npx hailbrowser-mcp trust --reset</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    To remove it: click <strong>Remove</strong> on the extension card, and delete the MCP entry from your
    client config. Chats, tasks and reports live in <code>chrome.storage.local</code> and go with the
    extension.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trouble">Troubleshooting</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause and fix</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>The panel says the extension is not connected</td><td>Open Chrome, check the panel's MCP label, click the pill to retry, and make sure the hub port matches in both places.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Port already in use</td><td>Something else owns 12306. Pick another port with <code>--port</code> and set the same port in Settings.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ollama returns 403</td><td>Start Ollama with <code>OLLAMA_ORIGINS=chrome-extension://*</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Chrome blocks the page</td><td><code>chrome://</code> pages and the Chrome Web Store cannot be automated. Navigate to a normal site.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A yellow "started debugging this browser" bar</td><td>Chrome showing that <code>chrome.debugger</code> is attached. It is expected and disappears when the tab closes or the extension reloads.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The agent clicks the wrong thing</td><td>Re-read the page first. Refs go stale after a navigation; <code>read_page</code> again instead of reusing an old <code>ref_N</code>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</Doc>
